From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] libwifi: a legacy client on an MLD AP is not an MLD
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

Fixes a second defect in 922. An AP-MLD also serves ordinary, non-MLO
clients, and 922 published the mlo_* fields for every station on an MLD link
regardless. map-agent then called stamld_update() for a legacy client and
stored it as a single-link MLD that is its own MLD.

Measured on bpi-4g, 2026-08-04. The controller held a sta_mld row for
b8:09:8a:50:f2:2a - a Mac reporting [HT][VHT] and no EHT - plus an
affiliated_sta row pointing at itself. In the mesh table that showed as two
MLO clients where only one of the three stations is actually MLO.

The discriminator is the affiliated address itself: hostapd reports
peer_addr[] only for a real MLO station. Measured on the same link socket:
three peer_addr lines for the MLO phone, none for the legacy Mac. So publish
the MLO block only when that lookup produced an address, and otherwise emit
plain macaddr + bssid, which is the truth about a legacy client.

A disconnect arriving after the mapping was lost - a wifimngr restart clears
it - now also publishes without the MLO fields. That is the safer of the two
failure modes: an incomplete event rather than an invented MLD.

Applies on top of 924-bss-entry-of-this-socket.patch.

--- a/libwifi/modules/wpactrl/wpactrl.c	2026-08-04 17:48:51.902733513 +0200
+++ b/libwifi/modules/wpactrl/wpactrl.c	2026-08-04 17:48:51.902777563 +0200
@@ -1047,23 +1047,49 @@
 	if (hapdctrl_sock_info(s))
 		return;
 
+	/* 925: an MLD AP also serves ordinary clients, and they are not MLDs.
+	 *
+	 * Emitting the mlo_* fields for every station on an MLD link made
+	 * map-agent call stamld_update() for a legacy client, which stored it
+	 * as a single-link MLD that is its own MLD. Measured on bpi-4g
+	 * 2026-08-04: the Mac (legacy, [HT][VHT], no EHT) had a sta_mld row of
+	 * its own and an affiliated_sta row pointing at itself.
+	 *
+	 * The discriminator is the affiliated address itself: "STA <mac>"
+	 * reports peer_addr[] only for a real MLO station - three lines for the
+	 * MLO phone, none for the legacy Mac. So publish the MLO block only
+	 * when that lookup actually produced an address, and otherwise emit a
+	 * plain macaddr + bssid, which is the truth about a legacy client.
+	 *
+	 * A disconnect after the mapping was lost (a wifimngr restart clears
+	 * it) also lands here and is published without the MLO fields. That is
+	 * the safer of the two failure modes: an incomplete event, not an
+	 * invented MLD.
+	 */
 	if (s->link_id >= 0) {
+		bool have_aff = false;
+
 		if (add) {
-			if (!hapdctrl_affiliated_mac(s, mld, aff))
+			if (hapdctrl_affiliated_mac(s, mld, aff) == 0) {
 				hapdctrl_stamap_store(s, mld, aff);
-			else if (hapdctrl_stamap_get(s, mld, aff, false))
-				memcpy(aff, mld, 6);
-		} else if (hapdctrl_stamap_get(s, mld, aff, true)) {
-			memcpy(aff, mld, 6);
+				have_aff = true;
+			} else if (hapdctrl_stamap_get(s, mld, aff, false) == 0) {
+				have_aff = true;
+			}
+		} else if (hapdctrl_stamap_get(s, mld, aff, true) == 0) {
+			have_aff = true;
 		}
-		sta = aff;
 
-		snprintf(mlo, sizeof(mlo),
-			 ", \"mlo_macaddr\":\"" HAPD_MACFMT "\""
-			 ", \"mlo_bssid\":\"" HAPD_MACFMT "\""
-			 ", \"mlo_link_id\":%d",
-			 HAPD_MACARG(mld), HAPD_MACARG(s->mld_addr),
-			 s->link_id);
+		if (have_aff) {
+			sta = aff;
+
+			snprintf(mlo, sizeof(mlo),
+				 ", \"mlo_macaddr\":\"" HAPD_MACFMT "\""
+				 ", \"mlo_bssid\":\"" HAPD_MACFMT "\""
+				 ", \"mlo_link_id\":%d",
+				 HAPD_MACARG(mld), HAPD_MACARG(s->mld_addr),
+				 s->link_id);
+		}
 	}
 
 	len = snprintf((char *)resp->data, resp->maxlen,
